# Architecture — how this app rides the platform

This repo contributes **one namespace** of workloads to a shared k3s cluster.
The platform (Traefik, cert-manager, external-dns, External Secrets Operator,
Prometheus/Loki, MetalLB) is shared; this repo supplies the app and its
per-namespace custom resources. Flux reconciles `kubernetes/flux/` into
`{{ app_namespace }}`.

## Request path

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  user(["User"]) -->|"{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}"| edge["Public DNS + edge"]
  edge --> lb["MetalLB VIP"]
  lb --> traefik["Traefik IngressRoute<br/>+ hsts-header{% if enable_sso %}<br/>+ authentik-auth{% endif %}"]
  traefik --> svc["Service :{{ app_port }}"]
  svc --> pod["App pod<br/>non-root, read-only rootfs"]
```

- **Public** `{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}`: the `IngressRoute` carries
  `external-dns.alpha.kubernetes.io/target: {{ external_domain }}`, so
  external-dns creates the record automatically. No operator action.
{%- if enable_internal_ingress %}
- **Internal** `{{ app_slug }}.{{ internal_domain }}`: reached only on the
  LAN/tailnet via the `lan-tailscale-only` middleware. The internal name is
  **not** auto-provisioned — the operator adds the record (ONBOARDING step O5).
  The middleware name is fixed cluster-side, so internal routes copy between
  tenant repos unchanged. On a cluster with no tailnet it allows the LAN and the
  pod CIDR and nothing else; the name keeps `tailscale` either way.
{%- if enable_sso %}
  SSO covers the **public** route only. This hostname is source-filtered to the
  LAN and tailnet, not authenticated: an Authentik proxy provider pins one
  `external_host`, so gating it too needs a second provider (ONBOARDING O8).
{%- endif %}
{%- else %}
- **Internal**: not published. `enable_internal_ingress` renders a second
  IngressRoute and its Certificate, plus one operator DNS step.
{%- endif %}
- **TLS**: a per-host `Certificate` (ClusterIssuer `letsencrypt-prod`, DNS-01)
  issues a Secret into this namespace, and Traefik terminates from it. Per-host
  rather than wildcard: Let's Encrypt caps duplicate certificates at 5 per week
  and the platform already issues the shared wildcards.
- **Middlewares** are referenced cross-namespace from the namespace that owns
  them: `hsts-header` from `traefik`{% if enable_sso %}, the SSO forward-auth `authentik-auth` from
  `authentik`{% endif %}. The cluster enables `allowCrossNamespace`.

## Secret flow
{% if secrets_backend == 'none' %}
This app declares no secrets. When it grows one, the shape is: an
`ExternalSecret` in this repo referencing the operator-owned
`ClusterSecretStore`, which pulls the value into a Kubernetes Secret that the
Deployment binds with `secretKeyRef`. Values never enter git.
{%- else %}
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  backend["{{ 'Vault items, prefixed with the slug' if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' else 'GitLab CI/CD variables' }}"]
  backend --> css["ClusterSecretStore<br/>{{ secrets_backend }}-{{ app_slug }}<br/>(operator-owned, namespace-scoped)"]
  css --> es["ExternalSecret<br/>(this repo)"]
  es --> sec[("Kubernetes Secret")]
  sec -->|secretKeyRef| pod["App pod"]
```

- Secret **values** never enter git. This repo holds only the `ExternalSecret`.
- The store is scoped to `{{ app_namespace }}` with `spec.conditions` — without
  it a `ClusterSecretStore` is readable from every namespace in the cluster.
{%- if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
- Items are prefixed with the slug in the shared vault
  (`remoteRef.key: "{{ app_slug }}: <Item>"`), which isolates them from other
  tenants' items.
{%- endif %}
- Keys are hyphenated, so the Deployment binds each one with `secretKeyRef`; an
  `envFrom: secretRef` would silently skip them (hyphens are not valid env-var
  names).
- The binding is not `optional:`. The pod stays in `CreateContainerConfigError`
  until the `ExternalSecret` has synced, so a mis-wired store fails visibly
  instead of starting a pod with no credential.
- The binding is read at container start. A rotated value reaches the app only
  after `kubectl delete pod -n {{ app_namespace }} -l app.kubernetes.io/name={{ app_slug }}`;
  the `ExternalSecret` reports Ready either way, and no alert shows the gap. A
  `rollout restart` is not a substitute: it writes a pod-template annotation
  the cluster's kustomize-controller reverts on its next reconcile.
{%- endif %}

## Storage & scheduling

The app is **stateless** as generated, and soft-avoids the storage node
(`{{ node_label_domain }}/nas`). This template ships no PVC and names no
StorageClass. Persistent data is an operator-assisted step: the operator
supplies the StorageClass or a pre-provisioned PV, and you add the PVC, the
volume and its mount. A volume added this way is outside every backup set until
the operator adds it, and its backup schedule is theirs. See ONBOARDING step O6.

The `/tmp` `emptyDir` is capped at 64Mi under a 512Mi container
`ephemeral-storage` limit. An app that uses `/tmp` for real work raises the
`sizeLimit` and keeps the limit above it, or the kubelet evicts the pod.

On a Proxmox/ZFS cluster the volume is a zvol and a matching PV, and the pod is
pinned to the storage node with a required hostname affinity plus a toleration
for `{{ node_label_domain }}/nas=true:PreferNoSchedule`. NFS-backed volumes
mount **by hostname** there: a wildcard certificate carries no IP SAN, so an IP
mount fails the TLS handshake.

## Network policy

`networkpolicy.yaml` denies all ingress and egress, then opens Traefik to the
app port{% if enable_servicemonitor %}, Prometheus to that same app port{% endif %}, cluster DNS, and public HTTPS. The public rule's
`except:` list is the RFC-reserved and private IPv4 space — a standards
constant, not this cluster's addressing — so the app cannot reach LAN, tailnet,
loopback or cloud-metadata addresses.

`scripts/check-netpol-except-parity.py` holds that list to the constant.
{%- if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %}
`task lint` runs it, the `netpol-check` job runs it, and so does the pre-commit
hook after `pre-commit install`.
{%- elif ci_shape == 'github' %}
`task lint` runs it, and so does the `manifest-gates` workflow on every pull
request and push to main. `pre-commit install` arms the same gate on every
commit.
{%- else %}
`task lint` runs it, and the pre-commit hook runs it on every commit once you
have run `pre-commit install`. This repo ships no pipeline, so those two are the
only gates.
{%- endif %}

A cluster whose LAN sits outside the shipped fence, or a rule that legitimately
needs peer-less egress, passes the gate a `--config FILE`. That file carries
`canonical_except_lists`, `fence_networks` and `unrestricted_egress_ok`. Add the
flag wherever the gate runs. That file replaces the built-in lists rather than
extending them, so a custom `canonical_except_lists` must still carry the set
the shipped `networkpolicy.yaml` uses (`reserved-full`), and `lan-fence` too if
you use it. An except-list matching no named set fails the gate.
`examples/netpol-except.example.yaml` is a filled-in copy of all three keys;
`load_config` in `scripts/check-netpol-except-parity.py` is the schema.

Anything else needs its own egress rule. Three worked examples:

Same-namespace traffic needs both halves: an egress rule here, and an ingress
rule on the peer, which `default-deny` fences too.

```yaml
# Egress to other pods in THIS namespace (a database or cache beside the app).
# The pod and Service CIDRs sit inside the 10.0.0.0/8 except above, so this
# traffic is denied until the rule exists.
- to:
    - podSelector: {}

# SMTP relay (submission).
- to: [{ipBlock: {cidr: <relay-ip>/32}}]
  ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 587}]

# kube-apiserver. kube-proxy DNATs the in-cluster VIP before NetworkPolicy is
# evaluated, so the SERVER NODE IPs are the allowlist — the ClusterIP never
# matches.
- to:
    - {ipBlock: {cidr: <server-node-ip>/32}}
  ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 6443}]
```

The peer's own policy carries the matching ingress half:

```yaml
policyTypes: [Ingress]
ingress:
  - from:
      - podSelector:
          matchLabels:
            app.kubernetes.io/name: {{ app_slug }}
    ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: <peer-port>}]
```

## Autoscaling & resilience

{% if enable_hpa -%}
- **HPA** owns the replica count on CPU (`minReplicas: 2`), so the Deployment
  ships no `replicas:`{% if enable_vpa %} and the **VPA** is memory-only — two autoscalers on one
  resource fight{% endif %}. Utilisation is measured against `requests.cpu`, which ships
  as the 25m placeholder, so raise it with the real image or the HPA sits at
  `maxReplicas`.
{%- else -%}
{% if enable_vpa -%}
- **VPA** (`updateMode: Initial`) right-sizes the pod on natural restarts,
  without evicting a running one. Set `updateMode: "Off"` for a stateful
  workload.
{% endif -%}
- Replicas are fixed at {{ replica_count }}.
{%- endif %}
{% if replica_count > 1 or enable_hpa -%}
- **PDB** (`minAvailable: 1`) keeps a replica up across coordinated node-reboot
  drains.
{%- else -%}
- **No PDB**: at a single replica `minAvailable: 1` blocks every voluntary
  eviction, and a node could then never drain.
{%- endif %}

## Observability

{% if enable_servicemonitor -%}
- **Metrics**: the `ServiceMonitor` is discovered cluster-wide by the platform
  Prometheus, and `networkpolicy.yaml` allows the scrape from the
  `observability` namespace on the same port.
- **Keeping `/metrics` private**: that port is the one the public `IngressRoute`
  backends, so metrics served there are readable from the internet. Give the
  container a second `containerPort`, add a second named port to `service.yaml`,
  then point `servicemonitor.yaml`'s `endpoints[].port` and
  `networkpolicy.yaml`'s `allow-scrape-from-observability` rule at that name.
  Excluding `/metrics` with a path condition on the public route works too.
{%- else -%}
- **Metrics**: not scraped. `enable_servicemonitor` renders the ServiceMonitor
  and the matching scrape NetworkPolicy together, once the app has a real
  `/metrics`.
{%- endif %}
- **Alerts**: the platform Prometheus discovers this `PrometheusRule` in every
  namespace. Every expression pins `namespace`, because tenant rules evaluate
  across the whole cluster. A `severity: info` rule also needs a `namespace`
  label of its own, set explicitly when the expression aggregates it away. The
  platform inhibits info alerts on an equal `namespace`, so an info alert
  without one is muted.
- **Reachability**: `AppDown` and `AppAbsent` read kube-state metrics, so they
  cover replica availability only. Certificates need no rule here: the platform
  already alerts on expiry and on a `Certificate` that never goes Ready, in
  every namespace. Do not re-declare those, because a tenant rule sharing an
  alertname at a different severity breaks the platform's routing. A broken
  route or DNS record fires nothing while the pod stays ready. End-to-end HTTP
  and DNS is an operator-added blackbox target (ONBOARDING step O7).
- **Logs**: container stdout/stderr ships to Loki via the platform's log agent.
  Nothing to configure.

## Local gates

`task lint` runs every gate this repo has. Three are worth knowing:

- `flux-lint` fails when kubeconform validated more resources against no schema
  than `ALLOWED_SKIPS` in `Taskfile.yml` allows. A CR your own operator defines
  is not in the public CRD catalog: add a `-schema-location` for it, or raise
  `ALLOWED_SKIPS`.
{%- if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %}
  The pipeline carries its own budget: raise it by passing `allowed_skips` to the
  `flux-lint` include in `.gitlab-ci.yml`, or that job still fails at zero.
{%- elif ci_shape == 'github' %}
  The pipeline reads its own budget from repository variable `ALLOWED_SKIPS`, so
  raise that too, or the `flux-lint` job still fails at zero.
{%- endif %}
- `scrape-wiring` fails when no NetworkPolicy admits the `observability`
  namespace on the port `servicemonitor.yaml` scrapes. A drifted pair is a
  `TargetDown` page in the platform's Alertmanager, not here. The gate models a
  namespace-scoped peer only: a peer that also carries a `podSelector` fails it,
  so keep the namespace peer whole and narrow elsewhere. A
  `spec.namespaceSelector` on the monitor may name this namespace
  (`matchNames: [{{ app_namespace }}]`); a wider scrape has to be checked where
  the policies covering it live.
{%- if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %}
  The `scrape-wiring-check` job runs the same script on every change.
{%- elif ci_shape == 'github' %}
  The `manifest-gates` workflow runs the same script on every change.
{%- else %}
  No CI job on this shape runs it. The pre-commit hook and `task lint` do.
{%- endif %}
- `kustomization` fails on an empty `resources:` list, on a listed path that
  does not exist, and on a manifest present but unlisted. An emptied list
  renders nothing and the cluster-side `Kustomization` then deletes every object
  this repo applied.
{%- if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %}
  The `kustomization-check` job runs the same script on every change.
{%- elif ci_shape == 'github' %}
  The `manifest-gates` workflow runs the same script on every change.
{%- else %}
  No CI job on this shape runs it. The pre-commit hook and `task lint` do.
{%- endif %}

## Platform contract

The rendered manifests bind to objects the cluster must already provide:

- A `ClusterIssuer` named `letsencrypt-prod`, for the per-host `Certificate`.
{%- if enable_internal_ingress %}
  Its DNS-01 solver must hold credentials for `{{ internal_domain }}` as well as
  `{{ external_domain }}`: the internal name is validated over public DNS even
  though it resolves only on the LAN.
{%- endif %}
- Traefik with `providers.kubernetesCRD.allowCrossNamespace: true`, and every
  middleware the routes name: `hsts-header`{% if enable_internal_ingress %} and `lan-tailscale-only`{% endif %} in `traefik`{% if enable_sso %},
  `authentik-auth` in `authentik`{% endif %}. Without the flag Traefik refuses the route and the
  hostname 404s.
- A Traefik `websecure` entryPoint. Every route here names it.
- external-dns watching the `traefik-proxy` source with the
  `external-dns.alpha.kubernetes.io` annotation prefix, which the public route's
  target annotation uses. Under a different prefix the annotation is ignored and
  no public record appears, while the pods stay ready and no alert fires. That
  annotation targets `{{ external_domain }}`, so the zone apex must itself
  resolve to the cluster's public ingress; an edge under a different name is a
  retarget in `kubernetes/flux/ingressroute.yaml`.
- A Prometheus that discovers `PrometheusRule`s{% if enable_servicemonitor %} and `ServiceMonitor`s{% endif %} in every
  namespace. Under the kube-prometheus-stack defaults
  `ruleSelectorNilUsesHelmValues: true`{% if enable_servicemonitor %} and
  `serviceMonitorSelectorNilUsesHelmValues: true`{% endif %} the selector is
  `matchLabels: {release: <release>}`, which these objects do not carry: set
  those inputs to `false` (the namespace selectors are already `{}`), or add the
  release label here. Left as they are, `AppDown` never
  fires{% if enable_servicemonitor %}, and the `ServiceMonitor` is never selected{% endif %}.
- kube-state-metrics, scraped by that Prometheus. `AppDown` and `AppAbsent` read
  `kube_deployment_status_replicas_available`; without it `AppAbsent` fires
  permanently and `AppDown` never fires.
- cert-manager controller metrics, scraped by that Prometheus. The platform's
  cluster-wide certificate alerts read them; without them a `Certificate` whose
  renewal fails is invisible until it expires.
- A cluster-scoped `tenant-crd-editor` ClusterRole granting edit on
  `traefik.io`, `monitoring.coreos.com`{% if enable_vpa %}, `autoscaling.k8s.io`{% endif %}{% if secrets_backend != 'none' %},
  `external-secrets.io`{% endif %} and `cert-manager.io`. The wiring file binds it. `admin`
  does not aggregate `traefik.io` or `monitoring.coreos.com`{% if enable_vpa %}, nor
  `autoscaling.k8s.io`{% endif %}; it normally covers{% if secrets_backend != 'none' %} `external-secrets.io` and{% endif %}
  `cert-manager.io` through their `*-edit` roles, which `tenant-crd-editor`
  repeats so a cluster missing one still applies them. A RoleBinding to a
  missing ClusterRole applies cleanly and grants nothing.
- The namespaces `traefik`{% if enable_servicemonitor %}, `observability`{% endif %} and `kube-system`, which
  `networkpolicy.yaml` names in its allow rules. The DNS rule ANDs the
  `kube-system` namespace with the pod label `k8s-app: kube-dns`, so on a cluster
  whose CoreDNS pods carry another label the peer matches nothing and the app
  resolves no names. Edit that rule's `podSelector`.
{%- if enable_vpa %}
- The `VerticalPodAutoscaler` CRD (`autoscaling.k8s.io`), the recommender, and
  the admission controller that applies its recommendation at pod creation.
- A resource-metrics API (metrics-server). Without it the VPA recommends
  nothing{% if enable_hpa %} and the HPA never leaves `minReplicas`{% endif %}, with no alert either way.
{%- elif enable_hpa %}
- A resource-metrics API (metrics-server). Without it the HPA never leaves
  `minReplicas`, and no alert says so.
{%- endif %}
{%- if secrets_backend != 'none' %}
- The External Secrets Operator, with the `external-secrets.io` `ExternalSecret`
  and `ClusterSecretStore` CRDs. `externalsecret.yaml` is inert without them.
{%- endif %}
{%- if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
- A `onepassword-connect` Service in namespace `external-secrets` on port 8080,
  which the `ClusterSecretStore` reads.
{%- endif %}
- A Flux `Kustomization` named `infrastructure-configs` in `flux-system`, or
  another name for the stage that applies the platform CRDs. The wiring file's
  `dependsOn` names it, and a name the cluster does not have leaves this repo's
  `Kustomization` retrying forever with nothing applied.
- Nothing here spells `${var}`: the wiring in ONBOARDING step O3 carries no
  `postBuild`. If the operator adds `postBuild.substituteFrom` so this repo can
  read the cluster's own domains and VIPs, kustomize-controller usually runs
  with `StrictPostBuildSubstitutions` on, and then an undefined `${var}`
  anywhere under `kubernetes/flux/` fails the whole tenant reconcile. A literal
  dollar-brace in a container arg, a config blob or dashboard JSON must be
  written `$${var}`.

## What the operator owns vs. what you own

| Operator (cluster repo) | You (this repo) |
|---|---|
| The `Namespace`, its PSA labels, its `ResourceQuota` and `LimitRange` | Everything under `kubernetes/flux/` |
| The `GitRepository` + `Kustomization` wiring | Workload, Service, routing CRs |
{%- if secrets_backend != 'none' %}
| The `ClusterSecretStore` + bootstrap secret | The `ExternalSecret` reference |
{%- endif %}
| Internal DNS records | Public routing (external-dns) |
| Platform middlewares, ClusterIssuer, VIPs | The per-host `Certificate` |
| Volume provisioning | PVC references, if any |
